    "id": 66,
    "firestoreId": "C5V0XcWhkMc5HHdv8Gor",
    "code": "lighr-cure-led",
    "name": "Lighr cure led",
    "arabicName": "Lighr cure led",
    "price": 980,
    "categoryKey": "clinical",
    "subCategoryKey": "operative-",
    "description": "LED Light Cure Unit\n\nAn LED Light Cure Unit is an optoelectronic device that emits a specific blue light to cure (harden) composite resin. It transforms the composite from a soft, moldable putty into a rock-hard restoration through a chemical process called photo-polymerization.\n1. Mechanism of Action: Why Blue Light?\n\nThe blue light is not just for show; it is selected for its specific wavelength, which typically ranges between 450 and 470 nanometers (nm).\n\n    The Photo-initiator: Inside dental composite, there is a light-sensitive chemical catalyst called Camphorquinone (CQ).\n\n    The Activation: Camphorquinone is chemically programmed to only absorb energy at that exact blue wavelength. When the blue light hits the CQ, it becomes excited and breaks down to release free radicals.\n\n    Polymerization: These free radicals trigger a chain reaction, forcing the small fluid molecules of the composite (monomers) to cross-link and bind tightly together into a solid, durable network (polymers).\n\n2. Why LED is the Industry Standard\n\nOlder generation curing lights used Halogen bulbs, but LED (Light Emitting Diode) units are now universally preferred because:\n\n    Cool Light Output: LEDs produce minimal heat compared to halogens. This protects the living tissue of the tooth (the dental pulp) from thermal damage and overheating.\n\n    Targeted Spectrum: LEDs emit light only within the useful blue spectrum needed by Camphorquinone, making them incredibly energy-efficient.\n\n    Ergonomics: They are cordless, lightweight, and powered by rechargeable batteries, making them highly portable in both the lab and clinic.\n\n3. Critical Clinical & Lab Rules for Light Curing\n\n    The Rule of Proximity (Distance):\n    The tip of your light guide must be as close to the composite surface as possible (ideally 1–2 mm away) without touching it. The intensity of light drops drastically with distance. If you hold the device too far away, the top layer of composite might look hard, but the bottom layers will remain soft and under-cured, leading to restoration failure and severe post-operative sensitivity for the patient.\n\n    Incremental Curing:\n    Most standard curing lights cannot penetrate deeper than 2 mm of composite effectively. Therefore, you must build and cure your restoration in increments (layers) of 2 mm or less.\n\n    Eye Protection is Mandatory:\n    Never look directly at the blue light with your naked eyes. Prolonged exposure causes cumulative damage to the retina (macular degeneration). Always look through the attached orange protective shield or wear safety goggles!",
    "arabicDescription": "LED Light Cure Unit\n\nAn LED Light Cure Unit is an optoelectronic device that emits a specific blue light to cure (harden) composite resin. It transforms the composite from a soft, moldable putty into a rock-hard restoration through a chemical process called photo-polymerization.\n1. Mechanism of Action: Why Blue Light?\n\nThe blue light is not just for show; it is selected for its specific wavelength, which typically ranges between 450 and 470 nanometers (nm).\n\n    The Photo-initiator: Inside dental composite, there is a light-sensitive chemical catalyst called Camphorquinone (CQ).\n\n    The Activation: Camphorquinone is chemically programmed to only absorb energy at that exact blue wavelength. When the blue light hits the CQ, it becomes excited and breaks down to release free radicals.\n\n    Polymerization: These free radicals trigger a chain reaction, forcing the small fluid molecules of the composite (monomers) to cross-link and bind tightly together into a solid, durable network (polymers).\n\n2. Why LED is the Industry Standard\n\nOlder generation curing lights used Halogen bulbs, but LED (Light Emitting Diode) units are now universally preferred because:\n\n    Cool Light Output: LEDs produce minimal heat compared to halogens. This protects the living tissue of the tooth (the dental pulp) from thermal damage and overheating.\n\n    Targeted Spectrum: LEDs emit light only within the useful blue spectrum needed by Camphorquinone, making them incredibly energy-efficient.\n\n    Ergonomics: They are cordless, lightweight, and powered by rechargeable batteries, making them highly portable in both the lab and clinic.\n\n3. Critical Clinical & Lab Rules for Light Curing\n\n    The Rule of Proximity (Distance):\n    The tip of your light guide must be as close to the composite surface as possible (ideally 1–2 mm away) without touching it. The intensity of light drops drastically with distance. If you hold the device too far away, the top layer of composite might look hard, but the bottom layers will remain soft and under-cured, leading to restoration failure and severe post-operative sensitivity for the patient.\n\n    Incremental Curing:\n    Most standard curing lights cannot penetrate deeper than 2 mm of composite effectively. Therefore, you must build and cure your restoration in increments (layers) of 2 mm or less.\n\n    Eye Protection is Mandatory:\n    Never look directly at the blue light with your naked eyes. Prolonged exposure causes cumulative damage to the retina (macular degeneration). Always look through the attached orange protective shield or wear safety goggles!",
