    "name": "Glass Slap",
    "arabicName": "Glass Slap",
    "price": 7,
    "categoryKey": "clinical",
    "subCategoryKey": "remove-",
    "description": "The Glass Slab is a thick, smooth, non-porous piece of glass used in dental clinics and labs as a mixing surface for various dental materials, especially cements and liners.\n1. Why do you need at least TWO (2) Glass Slabs?\n\nHolding at least two slabs in your kit is mandatory for a very important clinical reason: Cross-Contamination & Efficiency.\n\n    Material Separation: During a single clinical procedure (like placing a deep restoration), you often need to mix two different materials. For example:\n\n        Slab 1: Used to mix a cavity liner or base (like Zinc Oxide Eugenol - ZOE or Glass Ionomer Cement - GIC).\n\n        Slab 2: Kept completely clean and dry to mix a different luting cement, or kept ready if you need to redo a mix quickly without waiting to wash and dry the first one.\n\n    Temperature Management: If you are mixing a material that generates heat (Exothermic reaction) like Zinc Phosphate, the slab must be completely cold. Having a backup slab allows you to keep one in the clinic fridge while using the other.\n\n2. Primary Materials Mixed on a Glass Slab\n\n    Glass Ionomer Cement (GIC): Used as a base under composites or for luting crowns.\n\n    Zinc Oxide Eugenol (ZOE): Used as a temporary filling or sedative base.\n\n    Zinc Phosphate Cement: Used for luting/cementation.\n\n3. The \"Chilled Glass Slab\" Technique\nThis is a famous question in oral exams and a crucial trick for Zinc Phosphate and GIC:\n\n    The Concept: Before mixing, place your glass slab in the refrigerator (or under cold water, then dry it thoroughly).\n\n    The Benefit: The cold glass absorbs the heat generated by the chemical reaction (exothermic reaction). This slows down the setting time, giving you more working time to mix the powder and liquid perfectly and seat your restoration comfortably before it hardens.\n\n Critical Care Instructions for Your Kit:\n\n    Never scratch it: Always use a smooth stainless steel spatula (Cement Spatula) to mix. Avoid scratching the glass, because scratches will trap material and bacteria.\n\n    Clean immediately: Wipe the slab with a wet gauze immediately after mixing before the cement sets. Once materials like GIC or Zinc Phosphate harden on the glass, they are incredibly difficult to scrape off without scratching the slab.",
    "arabicDescription": "The Glass Slab is a thick, smooth, non-porous piece of glass used in dental clinics and labs as a mixing surface for various dental materials, especially cements and liners.\n1. Why do you need at least TWO (2) Glass Slabs?\n\nHolding at least two slabs in your kit is mandatory for a very important clinical reason: Cross-Contamination & Efficiency.\n\n    Material Separation: During a single clinical procedure (like placing a deep restoration), you often need to mix two different materials. For example:\n\n        Slab 1: Used to mix a cavity liner or base (like Zinc Oxide Eugenol - ZOE or Glass Ionomer Cement - GIC).\n\n        Slab 2: Kept completely clean and dry to mix a different luting cement, or kept ready if you need to redo a mix quickly without waiting to wash and dry the first one.\n\n    Temperature Management: If you are mixing a material that generates heat (Exothermic reaction) like Zinc Phosphate, the slab must be completely cold. Having a backup slab allows you to keep one in the clinic fridge while using the other.\n\n2. Primary Materials Mixed on a Glass Slab\n\n    Glass Ionomer Cement (GIC): Used as a base under composites or for luting crowns.\n\n    Zinc Oxide Eugenol (ZOE): Used as a temporary filling or sedative base.\n\n    Zinc Phosphate Cement: Used for luting/cementation.\n\n3. The \"Chilled Glass Slab\" Technique\nThis is a famous question in oral exams and a crucial trick for Zinc Phosphate and GIC:\n\n    The Concept: Before mixing, place your glass slab in the refrigerator (or under cold water, then dry it thoroughly).\n\n    The Benefit: The cold glass absorbs the heat generated by the chemical reaction (exothermic reaction). This slows down the setting time, giving you more working time to mix the powder and liquid perfectly and seat your restoration comfortably before it hardens.\n\n Critical Care Instructions for Your Kit:\n\n    Never scratch it: Always use a smooth stainless steel spatula (Cement Spatula) to mix. Avoid scratching the glass, because scratches will trap material and bacteria.\n\n    Clean immediately: Wipe the slab with a wet gauze immediately after mixing before the cement sets. Once materials like GIC or Zinc Phosphate harden on the glass, they are incredibly difficult to scrape off without scratching the slab.",
