    "price": 250,
    "categoryKey": "year-4-th",
    "subCategoryKey": "composite-",
    "description": "What you are talking about is the Composite Syringe (or compule), and using an expensive, high-end composite (like Filtek Z350 or Tetric N-Ceram) on a 2 EGP acrylic/plastic tooth is a total waste of money. Here is why your advice is spot-on, along with a few tips on picking that \"worst/cheapest\" type: Why cheap composite is actually better for practice: Bonding doesn't matter on plastic: Acrylic teeth don't have enamel or dentin, so you aren't actually etching and bonding them properly anyway. The composite is just staying in place purely by mechanical retention. No aesthetic demands: You don't need perfect shade matching, chameleon effects, or high translucency for a plastic jaw. A basic shade like A2 or A3 is all you need for every single tooth. Purely for handling skills: In 3rd year, you just want to practice how to pack the material, use your tools, adapt it to the cavity walls, and sculpt the anatomy (cusps and ridges) before light-curing. What to look for when buying it: When you go to the dental store, literally ask for the cheapest Chinese or local composite syringe they have. Brands like Sino-Dent, DenFil, or any generic/no-name Chinese microhybrid composite will cost a fraction of the price of premium brands. Bonus Tip: Sometimes cheap composites are actually stickier or harder to handle than expensive ones. If you can master sculpting and adapting a cheap, sticky composite on an acrylic tooth, working with a high-end, packable composite on a real patient in 4th year will feel like absolute butter! Save your money now, buy the cheapest stuff for the lab, and keep the premium kits for your real patients later",
