    "id": 311,
    "firestoreId": "zJQFQ9C4hVKT1cOR5hM0",
    "code": "wedge-",
    "name": "Wedge",
    "arabicName": "Wedge",
    "price": 180,
    "categoryKey": "year-3-th",
    "subCategoryKey": "operative",
    "description": "Let’s dive deep into the Wedge (الـ ويدج)! While it looks like a simple tiny piece of wood or plastic, it is arguably the most critical stabilizer in operative dentistry. Without it, even the best matrix bands or celluloid strips are useless.\n\nHere is the professional breakdown of how wedges work, their types, and how they interact with your matrix bands to create that perfect Proximal Wall.\nThe 3 Primary Roles of a Wedge\n\n    Gingival Seal (Preventing Overhangs):\n    The matrix band/celluloid strip naturally wants to flare out at the bottom (gingival floor). The wedge acts as a tight clamp, pressing the matrix firmly against the tooth structure. This completely seals the gingival margin and prevents composite from leaking out to create an overhang.\n\n    Tooth Separation (Ensuring Tight Contacts):\n    No matter how thin your matrix band or celluloid strip is (even if it's ultra-thin Russian Tor VM), it still takes up space. The wedge gently pushes the adjacent teeth microscopically apart. When you finish the restoration and remove the wedge, the teeth snap back into place, creating a perfectly tight, snapping contact point.\n\n    Matrix Stabilization:\n    It holds your band or celluloid strip completely still. During your J-Adaptation, you can pull the strip firmly without worrying about it slipping out of the gingival sulcus.\n\n Types of Wedges (Wood vs. Plastic)\n\nWhen you look in your clinic or lab kit, you will generally find two main types of wedges:\nA. Wooden Wedges (الـ ويدج الخشب)\n\n    How they work: Usually made of maple or sycamore wood.\n\n    The Magic Feature (Swelling): When a wooden wedge comes into contact with saliva, moisture, or blood in the crevicular fluid, it swells (expands). This swelling creates an even tighter, customized seal against the tooth.\n\n    Best Used For: Class II posterior restorations with metal matrix bands (like Tofflemire or Sectional matrices). They have great frictional grip and don't slip easily.\n\nB. Plastic / Diamond Wedges (الـ ويدج البلاستيك)\n\n    How they work: Made of flexible plastic, often with a hollow center or a diamond-shaped cutout.\n\n    The Magic Feature (Flexibility & Translucency): They can compress as they pass through the contact point and then expand once they are in the embrasure. Many of them are clear/transparent.\n\n    Best Used For: Class III & IV restorations using Celluloid Strips! Because they are clear, they allow the light from your curing unit to pass right through them, ensuring the composite at the deep gingival floor gets 100% polymerized.\n\nPro-Tips for Inserting a Wedge (Lab vs. Clinic)\n\n    The Correct Size Matter: Wedges are color-coded by size (e.g., Small, Medium, Large).\n\n        If it's too small, it won't seal the margin, and you will get an overhang.\n\n        If it's too large, it won't seat deep enough, it will push your matrix band inward, and it will flatten or cave in your proximal wall.\n\n    Insert from the Larger Embrasure: In 90% of cases, the lingual/palatal embrasure (the space between teeth on the inside) is much larger than the buccal/labial embrasure. Always try to insert your wedge from the lingual/palatal side for a better fit and stability.\n\n    Be Gentle on the Typodont: In the pre-clinical lab, forcing a large wedge between hard plastic teeth can crack the typodont gingiva or strip the screws holding the teeth. Choose a size that snugs in firmly but smoothly!",
    "arabicDescription": "Let’s dive deep into the Wedge (الـ ويدج)! While it looks like a simple tiny piece of wood or plastic, it is arguably the most critical stabilizer in operative dentistry. Without it, even the best matrix bands or celluloid strips are useless.\n\nHere is the professional breakdown of how wedges work, their types, and how they interact with your matrix bands to create that perfect Proximal Wall.\nThe 3 Primary Roles of a Wedge\n\n    Gingival Seal (Preventing Overhangs):\n    The matrix band/celluloid strip naturally wants to flare out at the bottom (gingival floor). The wedge acts as a tight clamp, pressing the matrix firmly against the tooth structure. This completely seals the gingival margin and prevents composite from leaking out to create an overhang.\n\n    Tooth Separation (Ensuring Tight Contacts):\n    No matter how thin your matrix band or celluloid strip is (even if it's ultra-thin Russian Tor VM), it still takes up space. The wedge gently pushes the adjacent teeth microscopically apart. When you finish the restoration and remove the wedge, the teeth snap back into place, creating a perfectly tight, snapping contact point.\n\n    Matrix Stabilization:\n    It holds your band or celluloid strip completely still. During your J-Adaptation, you can pull the strip firmly without worrying about it slipping out of the gingival sulcus.\n\n Types of Wedges (Wood vs. Plastic)\n\nWhen you look in your clinic or lab kit, you will generally find two main types of wedges:\nA. Wooden Wedges (الـ ويدج الخشب)\n\n    How they work: Usually made of maple or sycamore wood.\n\n    The Magic Feature (Swelling): When a wooden wedge comes into contact with saliva, moisture, or blood in the crevicular fluid, it swells (expands). This swelling creates an even tighter, customized seal against the tooth.\n\n    Best Used For: Class II posterior restorations with metal matrix bands (like Tofflemire or Sectional matrices). They have great frictional grip and don't slip easily.\n\nB. Plastic / Diamond Wedges (الـ ويدج البلاستيك)\n\n    How they work: Made of flexible plastic, often with a hollow center or a diamond-shaped cutout.\n\n    The Magic Feature (Flexibility & Translucency): They can compress as they pass through the contact point and then expand once they are in the embrasure. Many of them are clear/transparent.\n\n    Best Used For: Class III & IV restorations using Celluloid Strips! Because they are clear, they allow the light from your curing unit to pass right through them, ensuring the composite at the deep gingival floor gets 100% polymerized.\n\nPro-Tips for Inserting a Wedge (Lab vs. Clinic)\n\n    The Correct Size Matter: Wedges are color-coded by size (e.g., Small, Medium, Large).\n\n        If it's too small, it won't seal the margin, and you will get an overhang.\n\n        If it's too large, it won't seat deep enough, it will push your matrix band inward, and it will flatten or cave in your proximal wall.\n\n    Insert from the Larger Embrasure: In 90% of cases, the lingual/palatal embrasure (the space between teeth on the inside) is much larger than the buccal/labial embrasure. Always try to insert your wedge from the lingual/palatal side for a better fit and stability.\n\n    Be Gentle on the Typodont: In the pre-clinical lab, forcing a large wedge between hard plastic teeth can crack the typodont gingiva or strip the screws holding the teeth. Choose a size that snugs in firmly but smoothly!",
